{
  "teamId": 117,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Houston Astros were a team defined by resilience and frustration in equal measure. They stumbled out of the gate with a brutal 0-4 March and a 10-15 April, burdened early by a closer — Josh Hader — who seemed incapable of protecting leads when it mattered most. The bullpen's fragility was the story of the season's first half, with Hader surrendering multiple walk-off and game-altering home runs that cost Houston games they had no business losing.\n\nThen something shifted. Beginning in June and accelerating through August, the Astros caught fire. A 17-8 June — punctuated by Jake Meyers' three-run blast, Alex Bregman's clutch late-inning single, and contributions up and down the lineup — announced that this was still a dangerous club. The offense found its rhythm, the rotation steadied behind Framber Valdez, and the team posted a remarkable 18-10 August despite continued bullpen hiccups. Victor Caratini, Yainer Diaz, and Jose Altuve delivered in the clutch, propping up a lineup that quietly ranked among the American League's more productive units.\n\nThe back half of August told the story of a team that could win in spite of itself — Bryan Abreu's meltdowns against Baltimore and Kansas City were offset by timely singles from Diaz and a Altuve double that kept the Astros in the hunt. A 14-11 September secured the Wild Card berth, a minor miracle for a club that had looked rudderless in April.\n\nBut October brought a cruelly familiar ending. Matched up against the Detroit Tigers in the Wild Card round, the Astros managed just enough offense to keep games close but not enough to win them. Jeremy Peña's double plays and Josh Hader's inability to hold a late lead — an Andy Ibáñez double that plated three runs serving as the death blow — ended Houston's season in the first round for the second consecutive year. The 2024 Astros were a .547 team that overachieved its way into October only to be reminded that overachieving has its limits.",
